frame-shift-b0024b44.interpretation:
In this equation, $x'^{\mu}$ represents the four components of a spacetime event's position vector as observed in a new inertial reference frame, $S'$. The term $x^{
u}$ denotes the components of the same event's position 4-vector as observed in the initial reference frame, $S$. The Greek indices $\mu$ and $
u$ range from 0 to 3, corresponding to the spacetime coordinates (e.g., $x^0 = ct$, $x^1 = x$, $x^2 = y$, $x^3 = z$). The transformation is mediated by the Lorentz matrix $\Lambda^{\mu}_{
u}$, a $4 	imes 4$ matrix that encodes the specific boost and/or rotation relating the two frames. The equation utilizes the Einstein summation convention, where the repeated index $
u$ (one upper, one lower) implies a summation over its four values: $x'^{\mu} = \sum_{
u=0}^{3} \Lambda^{\mu}_{
u} x^{
u}$.

frame-shift-b0024b44.limits_and_boundary:
In the non-relativistic limit, where the relative velocity $v$ between frames is much less than the speed of light $c$ (i.e., $v \ll c$), the Lorentz transformation matrix $\Lambda^{\mu}_{
u}$ approximates the Galilean transformation. In this limit, time becomes absolute ($t' \approx t$) and spatial coordinates transform additively ($x' \approx x - vt$). Conversely, as $v 	o c$, the components of $\Lambda^{\mu}_{
u}$ involving the Lorentz factor $\gamma = 1/\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}$ diverge, signifying the extreme effects of time dilation and length contraction. If there is no relative motion or rotation, $\Lambda^{\mu}_{
u}$ becomes the identity matrix (represented by the Kronecker delta $\delta^{\mu}_{
u}$), and the coordinates remain unchanged: $x'^{\mu} = x^{\mu}$.

frame-shift-b0024b44.conceptual_definition:
This formula, $x'^{\mu} = \Lambda^{\mu}_{
u} x^{
u}$, is the defining property of a contravariant 4-vector in special relativity. It asserts that the components of a 4-vector $x'^{\mu}$ in a new inertial frame $S'$ are a linear combination of the components of the original 4-vector $x^{
u}$ in frame $S$. The coefficients of this linear transformation are the elements of the Lorentz transformation matrix $\Lambda^{\mu}_{
u}$. This rule ensures that physical laws written in terms of 4-vectors maintain their form (are covariant) under a change of inertial reference frame.
